# Give each client its own ssh host keys.
#
# The image carries one set, generated once when openssh-server was installed
# into it, so every client booting that image presents the same fingerprint.
# Two things follow from that, and neither is obvious until it bites:
#
#   - known_hosts cannot tell two clients apart. Every client is "the same
#     machine" as far as ssh is concerned, so a warning about a changed key
#     never appears, and neither does a warning about connecting to the wrong
#     one.
#
#   - the private halves travel to every client over NBD, in a file any of
#     them can read. A key that leaks from one client is the key for the whole
#     fleet, and rotating it means rebuilding and redeploying the image.
#
# Fresh keys per boot cost a fraction of a second and are thrown away with the
# session, which is the right shape for a machine whose root filesystem is
# reloaded from the network every time it starts. The price is that a client's
# fingerprint changes on every boot, so whatever connects to a client has to be
# told not to remember it:
#
#     ssh -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no -o UserKnownHostsFile=/dev/null root@client
#
# That is not a step down. Pinning a fingerprint that every client shares was
# never worth anything, and a client that reloads its entire root filesystem
# over unauthenticated TFTP and NBD is trusted on the strength of that boot
# chain, not on the strength of a host key.
#
# Only runs when sshd is going to run at all - 50-rm-system-services deletes
# the ssh unit unless KEEP_SYSTEM_SERVICES names it - so an image without a
# server pays nothing.
#
#     SSH_HOST_KEYS=image     keep the keys baked into the image
#
# Failure is never worth stopping a boot for: a client with no host keys is a
# client without ssh, which is what it had before openssh-server was added.

case " $SSH_HOST_KEYS " in
    *" image "*)
        return 0
        ;;
esac

[ -x /usr/sbin/sshd ] || return 0
command -v ssh-keygen >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0

case " $KEEP_SYSTEM_SERVICES " in
    *" ssh "*)
        ;;
    *)
        return 0
        ;;
esac

rm -f /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*_key /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*_key.pub
if ! ssh-keygen -A >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "ltsp: could not generate ssh host keys" >&2
fi
